{
  "dom_id": "step-q132448",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nI. The time until a laser fails, in hours, in a specific type of machine\n\nII. The number obtained when launching a fair roulette wheel\n\nIII. The number of children in a daycare center",
  "worked_solution": "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled using an exponential distribution. Laser failure in a particular machine can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the time it takes for the laser to fail.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate, and there is no interval of continuous time or space. Moreover, the number obtained when launching a roulette wheel is discrete, not continuous.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of children is discrete, not continuous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
